$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Al-Co-V
$
$ Not yet modelled.
$
$ B2 is quite a bit more dominating (AlCo-V) than experiment. Bcc does not
$ extend as much as it should. Co3V and sigma dissolve no Al. L1_2 is
$ present as a ternary phase at the correct location. When adding ternary
$ ordering parameters, L1_2 disappeared at 800 and 900 °C.
$
$PAR  G(FCC_4SL,AL:AL:CO:V:VA),,        +FAL2COV;,,                   N !
$PAR  G(FCC_4SL,CO:CO:AL:V:VA),,        +FALCO2V;,,                   N !
$PAR  G(FCC_4SL,V:V:AL:CO:VA),,         +FALCOV2;,,                   N !
$
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,AL:V:CO),,            +10*GHSERAL+4*GHSERVV
             +16*GBCCCO;,,                                            N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,CO:V:AL),,            +10*GFCCCO+4*GHSERVV
             +16*GBCCAL;,,                                            N Lin !
$
 FUNCTION U1ALCOV   298.15  0;                                         6000 N !
 FUNCTION U2ALCOV   298.15  0;                                         6000 N !
 FUNCTION U3ALCOV   298.15  0;                                         6000 N !
 FUNCTION FAL2COV   298.15  +2*UFALCO+2*UFALV+UFCOV+U1ALCOV;           6000 N !
 FUNCTION FALCO2V   298.15  +2*UFALCO+UFALV+2*UFCOV+U2ALCOV;           6000 N !
 FUNCTION FALCOV2   298.15  +UFALCO+2*UFALV+2*UFCOV+U3ALCOV;           6000 N !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Al-Cr-C
$
$ B. Hallstedt, D. Music, Z. Sun, Int. J. Mater. Res., 97, 539-42(2006).
$
$ Using the parameters from 08Con for bcc Al-C leads to some increase of the
$ C solubility in the Al-Cr bcc solid solution, but it remains very small
$ and is not visible in normal plots. The result is very reasonable.
$
$ Cr-C changed from 92Lee to 12Khv (only liquid changed).
$ Al-Cr changed from 98 Sau to 17Wan2.
$ Gibbs energy for Cr2AlC modified from 20Zhe.
$
$ At 1673 K the liquid extends less into the ternary when using the Gibbs
$ energy for Cr2AlC from 20Zhe. Cr2AlC2 is in equilibrium with Cr3C2 at
$ 1673 K. I.e.e Cr2AlC is more stable than in 06Hal.
$
$ The liquid using Al-Cr from 15Wit becomes much more stable towards high
$ temperature than when using 98Sau or 17Wan. Using the Gibbs energy for Cr2AlC
$ combined with Al-Cr from 17Wan results in a melting temperature of 1961 K
$ for Cr2AlC. This is much higher than the experimentally determined melting
$ temperature in the range of 1733 to 1768 K.
$
$ The stability of Cr2AlC was reduced to have a melting temperature of 1773 K,
$ which is close to the original melting temperature of 1771 K in 06Hal.
$ Cr2AlC is then not more in equilibrium with Cr3C2 at 1273 K.
$
$PAR  G(MAX_PHASE,CR:AL:C),,            -116710+413.51*T-69.3*T*LN(T)
$            -0.034913*T**2+263400*T**(-1);,,                         N 20Zhe !
 PAR  G(MAX_PHASE,CR:AL:C),,            -116710+420.25*T-69.3*T*LN(T)
             -0.034913*T**2+263400*T**(-1);,,                         N 23Hal4 !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Al-Cr-Fe
$
$ S. Wang, Z. Li, Z. Qin, S. Wang, X. Lu, C. Li, TMS 2017 Proc., 443-54(2017).
$
$ Checked against paper.
$
$ Compared to mod2 Al-Fe was changed from 09Sun to 17Zhe. This should only
$ cause minor changes.
$
$ The Al-Cr binary was further modified.
$ Compared to mod the fcc and hcp interactions, AL8CR5_D810 model,
$ AlCr2 stability and sigma (metastable) in Al-Cr have been changed.
$
$ The Al8Cr5-D810 parameters were completely redone. The Al-Fe binary
$ parameters are probably quite good. There is a Al8Cr5-D810 region on
$ the Al-poor side at 1433 K (no exp. data). Generally, Al8Cr5-D810 seems
$ a bit too stable towards the Al-poor (B2) side. Some further adjustment
$ of the ternary parameters would be needed for this.
$
$ This dataset is modified from 17Wan2 with the intention to have an acceptable
$ description of the Al-poor part of the system and an acceptable liquidus.
$ - With zero ternary interaction for Liquid and bcc the liquidus surface
$   for x(Al)<50 at.% from Rank 2019 is reasonably reproduced. Liquidus
$   temperatures seem generally to be a bit low by up to 30-40 K. For Al-rich
$   compositions the liquidus temperatures are higher than from the orginal
$   17Wan2 dataset.
$ - B2-AlCr taken from 01Dup (Al-Cr-Ni).
$ - Ternary B2 interactions and B2-CrFe tested, but only
$   L(B2_BCC,AL,CR:FE:VA;0) effective; larger value or L1 not effective.
$   L1 (and other interactions) has a strong effect on temperature for A2/B2
$   phase separation. Better fit requires optimization.
$ - The Al8M5-D82 interaction was essentially removed and the Al8Cr5-D810
$   parameters were slightly readjusted. The extension of Al8Cr5-D810 towards
$   Al-Fe is slightly less and there is a small Al8Cr5-D810 phase field at
$   1433 K, where it should not be stable.
$ - The sigma-D8B interaction was adjusted.
$ - According to Rank 2019 AlCr2 dissolves about 20 at.% Fe in the direction
$   AlFe at 973 K and is not present at 1173 K. The ternary phases O and D
$   are stable at 973 and 1173 K (in addition to H and E). These features
$   have not been considered in the present dataset.
$
$ This system should be completely reassessed. Considering the detailed work
$ by Rank et al. 2019 I expect the Hans Seifert group to do this within the
$ next few years.
$
$ The modelling from 17Wan2 concerns only the Al-rich (>50 at.% Al) part of the
$ system.
$
$ This modelling does not give a reliable impression.
$
$ G(AL7CR,AL:FE), G(AL8M5_D82,CR:FE), G(AL8M5_D82,FE:CR), G(ALCRFE_O1,AL:FE)
$ are missing in the paper.
$
$ The phase field for Al8M5-D82 (gamma-1) could not be quite correctly
$ reproduced. There is some
$ difference for the bcc field and some further minor differences.
$
$ Al8M5-D82 has a large phase field in the centre at 1673 K. This seems quite
$ improbable (and probably contradicts experiment). The liquidus at 1873 K
$ with Al8M5-D82 suspended) is not ok; bcc too stable.
$
$ There are contradictory reports for the crystal structures of the ternary
$ phases.
$
 PAR  L(LIQUID,AL,CR,FE;0),,            +ZERO;,,                      N 20Hal9 !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,AL:AL:CR:FE:VA),,       +FAL2CRFE;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,CR:CR:AL:FE:VA),,       +FALCR2FE;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,FE:FE:AL:CR:VA),,       +FALCRFE2;,,                  N Lin !
$
 PAR  L(B2_BCC,AL,CR:FE:VA;0),,         +10000;,,                     N 20Hal9 !
 PAR  L(B2_BCC,FE:AL,CR:VA;0),,         +10000;,,                     N 20Hal9 !
$
 PAR  L(AL7CR,AL:CR,FE;0),,             -115000;,,                    N 20Hal9 !
 PAR  L(AL11CR2,AL:CR,FE;0),,           -447300+100*T;,,              N 17Wan2 !
 PAR  L(AL4M_MU,AL:CR,FE;0),,           +ZERO;,,                      N 20Hal9 !
$
 PAR  G(GAMMA_D810,AL:CR:FE),,          +12*GHSERAL+5*GHSERCR
             +9*GHSERFE-500000-43*T;,,                                N 20Hal9 !
 PAR  G(GAMMA_D810,AL:FE:CR),,          +12*GHSERAL+5*GHSERFE
             +9*GHSERCR-500000+T;,,                                   N 20Hal9 !
 PAR  L(GAMMA_D810,AL:CR,FE:AL;0),,     -15000;,,                     N 20Hal9 !
 PAR  L(GAMMA_D810,AL:CR,FE:FE;0),,     -25000;,,                     N 20Hal9 !
 PAR  L(GAMMA_D810,AL:FE:AL,CR;0),,     -60000;,,                     N 20Hal9 !
$
 PAR  L(GAMMA_D82,AL:CR,FE;0),,         +17000;,,                     N 20Hal9 !
$
 PAR  L(M_AL13M4,AL:CR,FE:VA;0),,       +205868.77-330.98*T
             +0.13*T**2;,,                                            N 17Wan2 !
 PAR  L(AL5FE2,AL:CR,FE;0),,            -100000;,,                    N 17Wan2 !
 PAR  L(AL2FE,AL:CR,FE;0),,             +55545.66-66.72*T;,,          N 17Wan2 !
$
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,AL:CR:FE),,           +10*GHSERAL+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GHSERFE;,,                                           N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,FE:CR:AL),,           +10*GFCCFE+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GBCCAL;,,                                            N Lin !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,FE:CR:AL,CR;0),,      -1800000+600*T;,,             N 20Hal9 !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,FE:CR:AL,FE;0),,      -2000000+600*T;,,             N 20Hal9 !
$
 PAR  L(ALCRFE_O,AL:CR,FE;0),,          -90000;,,                     N 17Wan2 !
 PAR  L(ALCRFE_O,AL:CR,FE;1),,          -15000;,,                     N 17Wan2 !
 PAR  L(ALCRFE_O,AL:AL,CR,FE;0),,       +13634100-21640*T
            +8.49975*T**2;,,                                          N 17Wan2 !
$
 PAR  L(ALCRFE_D3,AL:CR,FE;0),,         +354134.409-691.54716*T
            +0.27127*T**2;,,                                          N 17Wan2 !
$
 PAR  G(ALCRFE_H,AL:CR:FE),,            +GHSERAL+0.09*GHSERCR
            +0.22*GHSERFE;,,                                          N 17Wan2 !
 PAR  L(ALCRFE_H,AL:CR:AL,FE;0),,       -41460+20*T;,,                N 17Wan2 !
 PAR  L(ALCRFE_H,AL:CR:CR,FE;0),,       -95390.9+33.3*T;,,            N 17Wan2 !
 PAR  L(ALCRFE_H,AL:CR:AL,CR,FE;0),,    -268000;,,                    N 17Wan2 !
$
 PAR  G(ALCRFE_E,AL:CR:FE),,            +0.77*GHSERAL+0.21*GHSERCR
            +0.03*GHSERFE-13290.5-1.5*T;,,                            N 17Wan2 !
$
 FUNCTION U1ALCRFE  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U2ALCRFE  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U3ALCRFE  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION FALCRFE2  298.15  +UFALCR+2*UFALFE+2*UFCRFE+U1ALCRFE;        6000 N !
 FUNCTION FALCR2FE  298.15  +2*UFALCR+UFALFE+2*UFCRFE+U2ALCRFE;        6000 N !
 FUNCTION FAL2CRFE  298.15  +2*UFALCR+2*UFALFE+UFCRFE+U3ALCRFE;        6000 N !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Al-Cr-Mn
$
$ S. Cui, I.-H. Jung, Metall. Mater. Trans. A, 48A, 1383-1401(2017).
$
$ Checked against paper.
$
$ Al-Cr is from 17Wan2 instead of 13Hu/17Cui1.
$ Al-Mn is still based on 07Du, but additions from 12Dju and 18Zhe2 instead of
$ 17Cui2.
$ The model for gamma-D82 is now 2sl 8:5 instead of 4sl 2:3:2:6.
$
$ With Al-Cr from 17Wan2 massive reworking is necessary because Gibbs energies,
$ particularly at high temperature, are rather different from 13Hu/17Cui1.
$ 17Wan2 is based on 08Lia and it is not clear whether 13Hu or 08Lia is the
$ better choice. As 17Wan2 has already been used for Al-Co-Cr and Al-Cr-Fe
$ it is used here as well. Maybe even a complete reassessment of Al-Cr should
$ be considered.
$
$ Interactions in Al-Cr changed for fcc, hcp, cbcc-A12 and cub-A13. Gibbs
$ energies for all Al-Mn countercompounds (in Al-Cr) changed. Cr solubility in
$ AL4MN_LAMBDA removed. The fcc interaction was readjusted to match the
$ Cr-solubility in fcc-Al from 17Cui1. The hcp, cbcc-A12 and cub-A13
$ interactions were then set equal to the fcc interaction. 
$ A large number of parameters have been removed. The final result is rather
$ different from result of 17Qui. The present result seems roughly acceptable
$ and some of the problems with the 17Cui dataset have been removed, but a
$ proper reassessment is needed.
$
$ Cui et al. use the MQM model for the liquid. In this dataset the RK model is
$ used. No attempt was made to fit liquidus data.
$
$ Ternary interactions are much too strong. The phases seem to be strongly
$ stabilized within the ternary, in particular in the Al-rich part. This
$ could lead to problems. A large negative liquid interaction is needed to
$ reproduce the liquidus in the Al-rich part (at least).
$ 
$ Hcp Cr-Mn interaction from 93Lee3 instead of 93Fri. 93Fri should be used.
$ 93Lee3 is used instead of 98Lee for the cbcc Cr-Mn interaction in mpea.
$
$ There is some misprint in the hcp interaction. The interaction is estimated
$ here. The Al-Cr and Cr-Mn hcp interactions are also changed.
$
$ The Al-solubility in both sigmas is too low.
$
$ G(GAMMA_D82,AL:MN:CR:AL) is missing in the parameter table.
$
 PAR  L(LIQUID,AL,CR,MN;0),,            +ZERO;,,                      N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,AL:AL:CR:MN:VA),,       +FAL2CRMN;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,CR:CR:AL:MN:VA),,       +FALCR2MN;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,MN:MN:AL:CR:VA),,       +FALCRMN2;,,                  N Lin !
$
 PAR  L(BCC_A2,AL,CR,MN:VA;0),,         +55000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(BCC_A2,AL,CR,MN:VA;1),,         +ZERO;,,                      N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(BCC_A2,AL,CR,MN:VA;2),,         -10000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  L(A2_BCC,AL,CR,MN:VA;0),,         +55000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(A2_BCC,AL,CR,MN:VA;1),,         +ZERO;,,                      N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(A2_BCC,AL,CR,MN:VA;2),,         -10000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  L(HCP_A3,AL,CR,MN:VA;0),,         +ZERO;,,                      N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(CUB_A13,AL,CR,MN:VA;0),,        -30000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  L(AL7CR,AL:CR,MN;0),,             -6000;,,                      N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(AL11CR2,AL:CR,MN;0),,           -20000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(ALCR2_C11B,AL:CR,MN;0),,        -15000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  L(AL12MN,AL:CR,MN;0),,            -25500;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(AL12MN,AL:CR,MN;1),,            -18000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  L(AL6MN_D2H,AL:CR,MN;0),,         -3500;,,                      N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  G(GAMMA_D810,AL:CR:MN),,          +12*GHSERAL+5*GHSERCR
             +9*GHSERMN-753000+206*T;,,                               N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  G(GAMMA_D810,AL:MN:CR),,          +12*GHSERAL+5*GHSERMN
             +9*GHSERCR-597000+139.4*T;,,                             N Lin !
 PAR  L(GAMMA_D810,AL:CR,MN:AL;0),,     -100000;,,                    N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  L(GAMMA_D82,AL:CR,MN;0),,         +20000;,,                     N 20Hal5 !
$
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,AL:CR:MN),,           +10*GHSERAL+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GBCCMN;,,                                            N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,MN:CR:AL),,           +10*GFCCMN+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GBCCAL-500000;,,                                     N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,MN:CR:AL,CR;0),,      -300000;,,                    N 20Hal5 !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,MN:CR:AL,MN;0),,      -300000;,,                    N 20Hal5 !
$
 FUNCTION U1ALCRMN  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U2ALCRMN  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U3ALCRMN  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION FALCRMN2  298.15  +UFALCR+2*UFALMN+2*UFCRMN+U1ALCRMN;        6000 N !
 FUNCTION FALCR2MN  298.15  +2*UFALCR+UFALMN+2*UFCRMN+U2ALCRMN;        6000 N !
 FUNCTION FAL2CRMN  298.15  +2*UFALCR+2*UFALMN+UFCRMN+U3ALCRMN;        6000 N !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Al-Cr-Ni
$
$ N. Dupin, I. Ansara, B. Sundman, Calphad, 25, 279-98(2001).
$
$ This is a rather major rework of the 01Dup dataset. Cr-Ni is changed from
$ 92Lee1 to 16Tan (already in 01Dup-4sl). Al-Cr is changed from 98Sau to
$ 17Wan. 17Wan is probably closer to 98Sau than the alternative 13Hu/16Wan.
$ Al-rich parts of the ternary are added/modified from 16Wan.
$
$ There only small shifts in the 75 at.% Ni section. After an adjustment of
$ the ternary bcc interaction the shift in B2/bcc is also rather small.
$ There are some changes in the Al-rich part. All three ternary phases are
$ stable at 1073, but only T1 (Al8Cr2Ni) at 973 K. T3 is just stable at 1173 K
$ where it should not be stable. The Ni solubility in Al8Cr5-D810 is similar to
$ 16Wan2 at 1273 K, but lower below that temperature.
$
 PAR  L(LIQUID,AL,CR,NI;0),,            +16000;,,                     N 01Dup !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_A1,AL,CR,NI:VA;0),,         +30300;,,                     N 01Dup !
 PAR  G(A1_FCC,AL,CR,NI:VA;0),,         +30300;,,                     N 01Dup !
$
 PAR  G(BCC_A2,AL,CR,NI:VA;0),,         +50000;,,                     N 20Hal6 !
 PAR  G(A2_BCC,AL,CR,NI:VA;0),,         +50000;,,                     N 20Hal6 !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,AL:AL:CR:NI:VA),,       +FAL2CRNI;,,                  N 01Dup !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,CR:CR:AL:NI:VA),,       +FALCR2NI;,,                  N 01Dup !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,NI:NI:AL:CR:VA),,       +FALCRNI2;,,                  N 01Dup !
$
 PAR  L(AL7CR,AL:CR,NI;0),,             -20000;,,                     N 20Hal6 !
$
 PAR  G(GAMMA_D810,AL:NI:CR),,          +12*GHSERAL+9*GHSERCR
             +5*GHSERNI-600000;,,                                     N 20Hal6 !
 PAR  G(GAMMA_D810,AL:CR:NI),,          +12*GHSERAL+5*GHSERCR
             +9*GHSERNI-600000;,,                                     N 20Hal6 !
$
 PAR  G(AL3NI2_D513,AL:CR:NI),,         +3*GBCCAL+2*GHSERCR+GBCCNI;,, N 16Wan2 !
 PAR  L(AL3NI2_D513,AL:CR,NI:VA;0),,    -80000+30*T;,,                N 16Wan2 !
$
 PAR  G(AL8CR2NI,AL:CR:NI),,            +8*GHSERAL+2*GHSERCR+GHSERNI
             -291000+23*T;,,                                          N 20Hal6 !
 PAR  G(AL8CR2NI,AL:NI:CR),,            +8*GHSERAL+GHSERCR
             +2*GHSERNI;,,                                            N 16Wan2 !
 PAR  G(AL8CR2NI,CR:AL:NI),,            +2*GHSERAL+8*GHSERCR
             +GHSERNI;,,                                              N 16Wan2 !
 PAR  G(AL8CR2NI,CR:NI:AL),,            +GHSERAL+8*GHSERCR
             +2*GHSERNI;,,                                            N 16Wan2 !
 PAR  G(AL8CR2NI,NI:AL:CR),,            +2*GHSERAL+GHSERCR
             +8*GHSERNI;,,                                            N 16Wan2 !
 PAR  G(AL8CR2NI,NI:CR:AL),,            +GHSERAL+2*GHSERCR
             +8*GHSERNI;,,                                            N 16Wan2 !
 PAR  L(AL8CR2NI,AL:AL,CR:NI;0),,       -20000;,,                     N 16Wan2 !
$
 PAR  G(ALCRNI_T2,AL:CR:NI),,           +0.8*GHSERAL+0.11*GHSERCR
             +0.09*GHSERNI-23490+2.3*T;,,                             N 20Hal6 !
 PAR  G(ALCRNI_T3,AL:CR:NI),,           +0.82*GHSERAL+0.15*GHSERCR
             +0.03*GHSERNI-15460;,,                                   N 20Hal6 !
$
$ Metastable
$
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,AL:CR:NI),,           +10*GHSERAL+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GBCCNI;,,                                            N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,AL:NI:CR),,           +10*GHSERAL+4*GBCCNI
             +16*GHSERCR;,,                                           N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,NI:CR:AL),,           +10*GHSERNI+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GBCCAL;,,                                            N Lin !
$
 FUNCTION FALCRNI2  298.15  +UFALCR+2*UFALNI+2*UFCRNI+6650;            6000 N !
 FUNCTION FALCR2NI  298.15  +2*UFALCR+UFALNI+2*UFCRNI;                 6000 N !
 FUNCTION FAL2CRNI  298.15  +2*UFALCR+2*UFALNI+UFCRNI;                 6000 N !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Al-Cr-V
$
$ Not yet modelled.
$
$ At 800 °C the system is bcc (except close to Al-Cr) below 40 at.% Al.
$ Gamma-D8_2 extends nicely across the system. There seem to be no experimental
$ data.
$
$ Metastable
$
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,AL:CR:V),,            +10*GHSERAL+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GHSERVV;,,                                           N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,AL:V:CR),,            +10*GHSERAL+4*GHSERVV
             +16*GHSERCR;,,                                           N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,V:CR:AL),,            +10*GFCCVV+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GBCCAL;,,                                            N Lin !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Al-Fe-C
$
$ F. Tang, PhD Thesis, 2018.
$
$ Modified from the 08Con dataset.
$
$ New Al-Fe from 17Zhe + further modifications.
$
$ Checked against thesis.
$
$ The kappa phase is modelled as a separate phase in contrast to Connetable
$ 2008, but with a much better model than Chin et al. 2010.
$
$ The Al-Fe system from Seiersten et al. 1993 has been
$ replaced with the new Al-Fe dataset from Sundman et al. 2009.
$ In the 1473 K section this leads to shift of the fcc + bcc + kappa
$ equilibrium to somewhat lower Al content in fcc and bcc.
$ At 1273 K this difference is very small, but the change of kappa from
$ 10Chi has the same effect, but more pronounced at 1273 K.
$
$ Al-Fe has been further modified by W. Zheng et al. 2017 for use in Al-Fe-Mn.
$ This causes an increased fcc stability.
$
$ The fcc interaction was modified to refit the fcc phase. The liquid
$ interaction was modified to better reproduce the graphite liquidus. This also
$ leads to higher eutectic temperatures. The bcc interaction in Al-C was
$ modified to slightly increase the C solubility in bcc.
$
$ The kappa parameters from 18Tan2 cause an inverse stability range of kappa
$ above 4270 K in Al-Fe. G(KAPPA_E21,FE:AL:VA) was changed to prevent this.
$
 PAR  L(LIQUID,AL,C,FE;0),,             -30000;,,                     N 20Hal2 !
$
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,AL,FE:C;0),,             -104000+80*T;,,               N 08Con !
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,AL,FE:C;1),,             +100000-40*T;,,               N 20Hal2 !
$
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,AL,FE:C;0),,             -104000+80*T;,,               N 08Con !
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,AL,FE:C;1),,             +100000-40*T;,,               N 20Hal2 !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,AL:AL:AL:FE:C),,        +ZERO;,,                      N 08Con !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,AL:AL:FE:FE:C),,        +ZERO;,,                      N 08Con !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,AL:FE:FE:FE:C),,        +ZERO;,,                      N 08Con !
$
 PAR  G(B2_BCC,FE:AL:C),,               +ZERO;,,                      N 08Con !
 PAR  G(B2_BCC,AL:FE:C),,               +ZERO;,,                      N 08Con !
$
 PAR  G(KAPPA_E21,AL:FE:C),,            +GHSERAL+3*GFCCFE+GHSERCC
             -115000+25.2*T;,,                                        N 10Chi !
 PAR  G(KAPPA_E21,FE:AL:C),,            +GHSERFE+3*GHSERAL+GHSERCC
             +238000-70.2*T;,,                                        N 18Tan2 !
 PAR  G(KAPPA_E21,AL:FE:C,VA;0),,       -15750+10.35*T;,,             N 18Tan2 !
 PAR  G(KAPPA_E21,AL:FE:C,VA;1),,       -82782+59.35*T;,,             N 18Tan2 !
 PAR  L(KAPPA_E21,AL,FE:FE:C;0),,       -20000;,,                     N 18Tan2 !
 PAR  L(KAPPA_E21,AL:AL,FE:C;0),,       -120000;,,                    N 18Tan2 !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Al-Fe-Mn
$
$ W. Zheng, H. Mao, X.-G. Lu, Y. He, L. Li, M. Selleby, J. Agren,
$ J. Alloys Compd., 742, 1046-57(2018).
$
$ Checked against paper and author's tdb (AlCFeMn_thermo_Zheng_2018).
$
$ Al13Fe4 parameters modified to be compatible with M-Al13Co4 in Al-Co-Mn.
$ Equilibria with Al13Fe4 change somewhat since the composition is somewhat
$ more Al-rich. G(AL13FE4,AL:MN:VA) should probably be more stable.
$
$ Based on the evaluation from 13Lin. The Fe-Mn-rich part is kept and the
$ Al-rich part is modelled here.
$
$ The tdb from Zheng contains both a 2-sl and a 4-sl bcc model. Results from
$ the two models are not identical. The figures in the 18Zhe2 paper appear
$ to have been calculated with the 2-sl model.
$
$ Slight change for equilibria with B2-bcc (compared to AlFeMn-18Zhe),
$ probably because of L11BALMN, which is zero in 18Zhe.
$
 PAR  L(LIQUID,AL,FE,MN;0),,            -14430-17.3*T;,,              N 18Zhe2 !
 PAR  L(LIQUID,AL,FE,MN;1),,            +ZERO;,,                      N 18Zhe2 !
 PAR  L(LIQUID,AL,FE,MN;2),,            +ZERO;,,                      N 18Zhe2 !
$
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,AL,FE,MN:VA;0),,         -22500;,,                     N 18Zhe2 !
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,AL,FE,MN:VA;1),,         +ZERO;,,                      N 18Zhe2 !
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,AL,FE,MN:VA;2),,         -31800;,,                     N 18Zhe2 !
$
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,AL,FE,MN:VA;0),,         -22500;,,                     N 18Zhe2 !
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,AL,FE,MN:VA;1),,         +ZERO;,,                      N 18Zhe2 !
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,AL,FE,MN:VA;2),,         -31800;,,                     N 18Zhe2 !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,AL:AL:FE:MN:VA),,       +FAL2FEMN;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,FE:FE:AL:MN:VA),,       +FALFE2MN;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,MN:MN:AL:FE:VA),,       +FALFEMN2;,,                  N Lin !
$
 PAR  L(BCC_A2,AL,FE,MN:VA;0),,         +ZERO;,,                      N 13Lin !
