$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Co-Ni-V
$
$ W.-M. Choi, Y.H. Jo, D.G. Kim, S.S. Sohn, S. Lee, B.-J. Lee,
$ Calphad, 66, 101624(2019).
$
$ Checked against paper and author's tdb.
$
$ Sigma changed from 8:4:18 to 10:4:16.
$ Ni included on the 2nd sl in sigma --> several new parameters.
$ Added fcc and bcc ordering.
$ CoV3 and NiV3 merged to one phase (MV3_A15).
$ Co-V changed from 04Hua to 20Wan-mod.
$ Ni-V changed from 98Kor to 19Noo.
$
$ The two new binaries are rather different from the previously used ones and
$ cause large changes in the ternary. The Gibbs energies are considerably
$ larger and, for fcc and bcc, much more asymmetric. The asymmetry has
$ considerable effect inside the ternary due to the RKM extrapolation.
$
$ A rather large number of substantial parameters were needed to get an
$ acceptable result. This system should be properly optimised, possibly
$ including some modifications in the Co-V and Ni-V binaries.
$
$ For the additional sigma parameters, a linear change from Ni-V to Co-V
$ was assumed, using ab initio values from 06Slu and 20Wan for the Co-V
$ en points. Eventually Co should also be included on the 2nd sl, but this
$ generates even more parameters.
$
$ Co3V and Ni3V are modelled as one phase, although they have different crystal
$ structures.
$
$ An equiatomic alloy was investigated by S.S. Sohn et al. (Acta Mater., 194,
$ 106-17(2020)) and found to be fcc at 1173 K and M3V + sigma at 1073 K.
$ The M3V phase was found to have the BaPb3 structure (hR12, R-3m). This is
$ presumably a stacking variant of the Al3Pu structure found for Co3V. See
$ further references within Sohn et al.
$
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,CO,NI,V:VA;0),,          -27500+10*T;,,                N 21Hal5 !
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,CO,NI,V:VA;1),,          -27500+10*T;,,                N 21Hal5 !
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,CO,NI,V:VA;2),,          -27500+10*T-30000;,,          N 21Hal5 !
$
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,CO,NI,V:VA;0),,          -27500+10*T;,,                N 21Hal5 !
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,CO,NI,V:VA;1),,          -27500+10*T;,,                N 21Hal5 !
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,CO,NI,V:VA;2),,          -27500+10*T-30000;,,          N 21Hal5 !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,CO:CO:NI:V:VA),,        +FCO2NIV;,,                   N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,NI:NI:CO:V:VA),,        +FCONI2V;,,                   N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,V:V:CO:NI:VA),,         +FCONIV2;,,                   N Lin !
$
$PAR  L(M3V,CO,NI:V;0),,                +7000;,,                      N 19Cho !
 PAR  L(M3V,CO,NI:V;0),,                +30000;,,                     N 21Hal5 !
 PAR  L(M3V,CO,NI:V;1),,                -5000;,,                      N 21Hal5 !
 PAR  L(M3V,CO,NI,V:V;0),,              +80000;,,                     N 21Hal5 !
$
 PAR  L(MV3_A15,CO,NI:V;0),,            +5000;,,                      N 21Hal5 !
$
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,CO:NI:V),,            +10*GHSERCO+4*GHSERNI
             +16*GHSERVV-377380;,,                                    N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,CO:V:NI),,            +0.3846*GSCOVCO
             +0.6154*GSNIVNI;,,                                       N 19Cho !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,NI:V:CO),,            +0.3846*GSNIVNI
             +0.6154*GSCOVCO;,,                                       N 19Cho !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,V:NI:CO),,            +10*GHSERVV+4*GHSERNI
             +16*GHSERCO+55543;,,                                     N Lin !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,CO:V:NI,V;0),,        -200000;,,                    N 21Hal5 !
$
 FUNCTION U1CONIV   298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U2CONIV   298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U3CONIV   298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCONIV2   298.15  +UFCONI+2*UFCOV+2*UFNIV+U1CONIV;           6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCONI2V   298.15  +2*UFCONI+UFCOV+2*UFNIV+U2CONIV;           6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCO2NIV   298.15  +2*UFCONI+2*UFCOV+UFNIV+U3CONIV;           6000 N !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Co-V-C
$
$ S. Huang, L. Li, O. Van der Biest, J. Vleugels,
$ J. Alloys Compd., 385, 114-18(2004).
$
$ Checked against paper.
$
$ This is just an extrapolation. There were no experimental data and no
$ parameters were added.
$
$ Co-V changed from 04Hua to 20Wan-mod.
$ V-C changed from 91Hua to 20Hua (minor modification of 91Hua).
$
$ The change of Co-V leads to large changes in the ternary. The VC solubility
$ in fcc-Co increases considerable. The is a small liquid field at 1573 K
$ close to the Co-C binary (which is not present in the original version).
$ At 1773 K the VC solubility in the Co-based liquid is very much larger.
$
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
