$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Cr-Fe-Mn
$
$ B.-J. Lee, Metall. Trans. A, 24A, 1919-33(1993).
$
$ Checked against paper.
$
$ Changed model for sigma from 8:4:18 to 10:4:16.
$ Ternary parameters were recalculated using the scheme in 93Lee3 with a
$ modified correction term; -135000 J instead of -123570 J.
$
$ The sigma phase field is very close to that in 93Lee3.
$
$ There are quite large differences at high temperature when high-sigma is
$ removed. This is in conflict with experimental data from J. Ruan et al. 2021.
$ (Materialia, 20, 101231(2021)) at 1323 and 1473 K where they found a large
$ sigma/high-sigma region.
$
$ The sigma phase was further adjusted and a ternary interaction for cub-A13
$ included. The 1323 K isothermal section from Ruan is now reasonably
$ reproduced. At 1473 K cub-A13 is stable, but not sigma-phase.
$ At 1123 K there are only minor differences compared to 93Lee. The cub-A13
$ phase field is larger. At 1073 K the cbcc-A12 phase field is replaced by
$ cub-A13. The sigma phase field is somewhat more narrow.
$
 PAR  L(LIQUID,CR,FE,MN;0),,            +2378;,,                      N 93Lee3 !
$
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,CR,FE,MN:VA;0),,         +6715-10.3933*T;,,            N 93Lee3 !
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,CR,FE,MN:VA;0),,         +6715-10.3933*T;,,            N 93Lee3 !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,CR:CR:FE:MN:VA),,       +FCR2FEMN;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,FE:FE:CR:MN:VA),,       +FCRFE2MN;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,MN:MN:CR:FE:VA),,       +FCRFEMN2;,,                  N Lin !
$
 PAR  L(BCC_A2,CR,FE,MN:VA;0),,         -5996;,,                      N 93Lee3 !
 PAR  L(A2_BCC,CR,FE,MN:VA;0),,         -5996;,,                      N 93Lee3 !
$
 PAR  L(CUB_A13,CR,FE,MN:VA;0),,        -30000;,,                     N 23Hal2 !
$
$PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,FE:CR:MN),,           +10*GFCCFE+4*GHSERCR
$            +16*GBCCMN-49718-2.446*T;,,                              N 16Hal6 !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,FE:CR:MN),,           +10*GFCCFE+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GBCCMN-16028-30*T;,,                                 N 23Hal2 !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,MN:CR:FE),,           +10*GFCCMN+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GHSERFE-113380-43.274*T;,,                           N 16Hal6 !
$PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,FE:CR:CR,MN;0),,      -947617+762.6*T;,,            N 16Hal6 !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,FE:CR:CR,MN;0),,      -428100+300*T;,,              N 23Hal2 !
$
$ Metastable
$
 PAR  L(HCP_A3,CR,FE,MN:VA;0),,         +34600;,,                     N 93Qiu !
$
 FUNCTION U1CRFEMN  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U2CRFEMN  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U3CRFEMN  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCRFEMN2  298.15  +UFCRFE+2*UFCRMN+2*UFFEMN+U1CRFEMN;        6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCRFE2MN  298.15  +2*UFCRFE+UFCRMN+2*UFFEMN+U2CRFEMN;        6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCR2FEMN  298.15  +2*UFCRFE+2*UFCRMN+UFFEMN+U3CRFEMN;        6000 N !
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
$ Cr-Fe-Ni
$
$ B.-J. Lee, J. Korean Inst. Met. Mater., 31, 480-89(1993).
$
$ Checked against paper (93Lee4 and 90Hil).
$
$ Cr-Ni is changed from 92Lee1 to 16Tan.
$ Fe-Ni is changed from 03Dup to 10Cac.
$
$ The change of Fe-Ni causes small but visible changes in fcc-bcc-liquid
$ equilibria. The sigma phase extends less far into the ternary and is not
$ stable at 1193 K any more. This was solved by adding a ternary interaction,
$ but probably the Ni:Cr:Cr parameter should be adjusted instead. This would
$ require checking (and possible changes) in other Cr-Ni-X systems with
$ sigma phase.
$
$ Sigma phase model is changed from 8:4:18 to 10:4:16.
$ Ni:Cr:Cr parameter adjusted for better fit in Cr-Fe-Ni.
$
$ Results are very similar to those of 93Lee4, including the 74 wt.% Fe
$ isopleth. There are small shifts in fcc, bcc, liquid and sigma phase
$ boundaries.
$
$ Fcc and bcc ordering is added; 4SL fcc and 2SL bcc with Va.
$ Fcc ordering was also added by P. Franke 2011 (based on Fe-Ni from 03Dup).
$
$ Ordering does not behave well at high temperature (similar to the
$ Fe-Mn-Ni system). At 1673 K L1_2 is stable as (Fe,Ni)3Cr, from around
$ Fe2NiCr to FeNi2Cr. The 823 K isothermal section is correctly reproduced
$ with no ordered phases. Results at 773 and 673 K are similar to the
$ the results P. Franke 2011. The high temperature problem was solved by
$ changing the UFFENI parameter in the Fe-Ni system.
$
 PAR  L(LIQUID,CR,FE,NI;0),,            +36583;,,                     N 93Lee1 !
 PAR  L(LIQUID,CR,FE,NI;1),,            +13254;,,                     N 93Lee1 !
 PAR  L(LIQUID,CR,FE,NI;2),,            -10018;,,                     N 93Lee1 !
$
 PAR  L(FCC_A1,CR,FE,NI:VA;0),,         +16580-9.7835*T;,,            N 93Lee1 !
 PAR  L(A1_FCC,CR,FE,NI:VA;0),,         +16580-9.7835*T;,,            N 93Lee1 !
$
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,CR:CR:FE:NI:VA),,       +FCR2FENI;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,FE:FE:CR:NI:VA),,       +FCRFE2NI;,,                  N Lin !
 PAR  G(FCC_4SL,NI:NI:CR:FE:VA),,       +FCRFENI2;,,                  N Lin !
$
 PAR  L(BCC_A2,CR,FE,NI:VA;0),,         -2673+2.04145*T;,,            N 93Lee1 !
 PAR  L(A2_BCC,CR,FE,NI:VA;0),,         -2673+2.04145*T;,,            N 93Lee1 !
$
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,FE:CR:NI),,           +10*GFCCFE+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GBCCNI;,,                                            N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,FE:NI:CR),,           +10*GFCCFE+4*GBCCNI
             +16*GHSERCR;,,                                           N Lin !
 PAR  G(SIGMA_D8B,NI:CR:FE),,           +10*GHSERNI+4*GHSERCR
             +16*GHSERFE;,,                                           N Lin !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,FE,NI:CR:CR;0),,      -20000;,,                     N 21Hal8 !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,FE,NI:CR:FE;0),,      -20000;,,                     N 21Hal8 !
 PAR  L(SIGMA_D8B,FE,NI:CR:NI;0),,      -20000;,,                     N 21Hal8 !
$
 FUNCTION U1CRFENI  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U2CRFENI  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION U3CRFENI  298.15  0.0;                                       6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCRFENI2  298.15  +UFCRFE+2*UFCRNI+2*UFFENI+U1CRFENI;        6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCRFE2NI  298.15  +2*UFCRFE+UFCRNI+2*UFFENI+U2CRFENI;        6000 N !
 FUNCTION FCR2FENI  298.15  +2*UFCRFE+2*UFCRNI+UFFENI+U3CRFENI;        6000 N !
